@@ -69,8 +69,8 @@ reliably identify unstable.
 
 ## Existing imports and generated configurations
 
-Existing underscore exports such as `nixosModules.container_24_11` keep their
-exact release-specific behavior. The legacy exports
+Existing underscore exports such as `nixosModules.container_24_11` continue to
+import the named release module. The legacy exports
 `nixosConfigurations.container` and `nixosConfigurations.containerStable` keep
 using the raw latest-stable module, independently of the guest release.
 `nixosConfigurations.containerUnstable` remains the explicit unstable module.
@@ -127,18 +127,34 @@ its early mount path. User allocation must see the saved maps before it runs;
 mounting them later leaves newly allocated IDs in `/etc/passwd` and `/etc/group`
 while exposing the original maps and file ownership on persistent storage.
 
-The compatibility step prepares absent backing directories and checks the
-mount before allowing allocation. Its source must be below an available mount
-separate from `/`; the supplied images use the host-mounted `/persistent`
-dataset. Custom root-backed sources are rejected, including layouts that
-previously activated successfully. Provision sources on separate backing
-storage before adopting the correction. Missing storage or a destination
-already mounted from a different source aborts activation before allocation.
-The step preserves existing content and metadata; absent directories receive
-root ownership and mode 0755. Custom sources requiring different ownership
-before allocation must be provisioned beforehand. Impermanence retains its
-normal later ownership and mode handling. Sysusers and userborn keep their
-existing activation paths.
+The compatibility module prepares absent source directories when NixOS mounts
+the selected binds, using the normal global mount order. It checks the bind
+before allowing allocation. Existing content and metadata remain untouched;
+absent directories receive root ownership and mode 0755. Provision custom
+sources beforehand if they need different ownership before allocation.
+Impermanence retains its later ownership and mode handling.
+
+Sources can reside on persistent `/` or on a separate backing mount. The supplied
+images use the host-mounted `/persistent` dataset; guests need no block device
+or storage-layout change. For backing mounts declared in NixOS's early mount
+set, activation checks that the covering mountpoints are present before
+creating source directories. Declared directory binds must also match their
+configured source directory. A failed required backing mount, failed selected
+bind, or destination mounted from a different source stops activation before
+legacy allocation. Matching live binds retain their source and configured
+remount options.
+
+Host-supplied storage that has no guest early-mount declaration must already be
+available. The guest cannot distinguish an intentionally root-backed directory
+from stale directories exposed when an undeclared host mount is missing. Mount
+presence and directory identity do not prove that storage will survive a host
+failure.
+
+A healthy guest can adopt by updating its vpsAdminOS input or downloaded module
+and rebuilding with its existing nixpkgs pin, stateVersion and storage layout.
+Guests adopt independently without host coordination. Sysusers and userborn
+keep their existing activation paths. An explicit guest replacement of the
+specialfs activation script remains responsible for its own mount behavior.
 
 This preserves identities in healthy guests. It does not recover a guest whose
 UIDs or GIDs have already changed.
